<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Didrikson Zaharias called the oncology line three days into a cough and was seen the same day. She had three doses of dostarlimab, the last two weeks ago, and cycle 4 is due in a week. The illness began with a scratchy throat and a runny nose after a tournament week in which several players in the locker room had colds. She now has a dry cough, worse at night, and a temperature up to 37.9 °C at home. She is not short of breath, even climbing the slope to the eighteenth green, and has no chest pain. She has no diarrhea, rash, or abdominal pain, and her bowel movements have been normal since the second dose, with no blood. She has felt more tired than usual for about two weeks, before the cold began. She is practicing but skipped yesterday&apos;s round. She has had no thoughts of self-harm. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Three days ago, after colds among players at a tournament</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Dry cough, worse at night; temperature up to 37.9 °C; not short of breath</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Three doses of dostarlimab; a new cough on PD-1 blockade must prompt a check for pneumonitis</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Runny nose and sore throat; tired for two weeks; no diarrhea, rash or chest pain</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

