<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral upper respiratory infection (J06.9)</content>. A viral cold with cough after exposure on tour. On PD-1 blockade a new cough must raise the question of pneumonitis, but her oxygen saturation is normal at rest and on exertion, the lungs are clear and the chest X-ray is clear, so pneumonitis is unlikely (grade 0). Rapid influenza and COVID-19 tests were negative. No antibiotic is needed. [S]<list><item>Fluids, rest, honey and warm drinks for the cough; acetaminophen for fever.</item><item>Call at once for breathlessness, oxygen saturation below 94% on her home oximeter, chest pain or fever above 38.5 °C; any of these would need a CT of the chest.</item><item>Cycle 4 next week if the fever has gone.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Mismatch repair-deficient low rectal cancer on dostarlimab (C20)</content>. Three doses given; bleeding has stopped and bowel habit is normal. The two weeks of tiredness before the cold led to a thyroid check today: TSH 5.8 mIU/L with a normal free T4 of 1.0 ng/dL, a mild rise from 2.2 at the last visit, which can be the start of immune-related thyroiditis. No treatment is needed at this level. [S]<list><item>TSH and free T4 again before cycle 5, with the restaging visit.</item><item>Restaging MRI and sigmoidoscopy as booked.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Rectal Cancer (current version); summarized in Benson AB, et al. NCCN Guidelines Insights: Rectal Cancer, Version 3.2024. J Natl Compr Canc Netw 2024;22(6):366-375 (PMID 39151454) (2026); Schneider BJ, et al. Management of Immune-Related Adverse Events in Patients Treated With Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy: ASCO Guideline Update. J Clin Oncol 2021;39(36):4073-4126 (PMID 34724392) (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Cycle 4 in one week if afebrile. Restaging and review at cycle 5. Call for breathlessness, chest pain, diarrhea, rash or high fever. Next scheduled visit 1953-06-29: restaging after four doses: pelvic MRI and endoscopy, immune-related hypothyroidism, and the decision to continue PD-1 blockade. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
